<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/evolution-natural-selection">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Evolution by natural selection is biology's unifying theory — the frame that makes every
    other fact in the subject make sense. Why does the orchid's flower fit its moth exactly?
    Why does the antibiotic that worked in 1990 fail in 2025? Why are there whales with
    pelvises, finches with thirteen slightly different beaks, and humans who cannot make
    their own vitamin C? One answer covers all of it. Darwin published the mechanism in 1859
    (<em>On the Origin of Species</em>) after two decades of caution, and it is four plain
    steps with one deep consequence: <strong>populations change over generations because the
    environment filters reproduction</strong>. No foresight, no purpose, no ladder of
    progress — just differential survival running on heritable variation, compounding for
    billions of years. It explains both the diversity of life and the perfect fit of
    organisms to their niches, without any design being required at any step.
  </p>
  <p>
    The syllabus breaks the mechanism into its three working parts — variation, adaptive
    features, selection — and this lesson follows that order: where the raw differences come
    from, what a "good fit" actually is, and how the filter turns differences into change.
  </p>

  <h2>Variation: the raw material</h2>
  <p>
    Look around any classroom: everyone is a different height, with different blood groups,
    different nose shapes. <strong>Variation</strong> is universal, and it comes in two
    flavours. <strong>Continuous</strong> variation — height, weight, skin tone, leaf length
    — shows a smooth range of values, because many genes plus environment contribute.
    <strong>Discontinuous</strong> variation — blood group, tongue-rolling, seed shape —
    falls into distinct categories set by one or a few genes. The deeper cut separates
    <strong>genetic</strong> variation (written in DNA, inherited, and therefore the only
    kind selection can act on) from <strong>environmental</strong> variation (a well-fed
    child is taller, but their children inherit genes, not dinners). Only the genetic kind
    is evolution's currency.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where does genetic variation come from? Two engines. <strong>Mutation</strong> —
    spontaneous changes in DNA from copying errors, radiation or chemicals — creates
    genuinely new alleles; it is rare per gene and usually neutral or harmful, but across
    billions of cell divisions it is a constant drizzle of novelty (a human child carries
    roughly 60–100 new mutations). <strong>Sexual reproduction</strong> reshuffles existing
    alleles: meiosis independently assorts chromosomes and crosses them over, and
    fertilisation combines two shuffled decks — which is why no two offspring of the same
    parents (identical twins excepted) are alike, and why asexual species' variation comes
    only from mutation. A population's total allele variety is its
    <strong>gene pool</strong>, and its size matters biologically: small, inbred populations
    (cheetahs, island species) carry little variation, which is precisely what leaves them
    unable to adapt when disease or climate shifts.
  </p>

  <h2>Adaptive features: what a good fit looks like</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>adaptation</strong> is any inherited feature that increases an organism's
    chances of surviving and reproducing in its environment — and the fit can be studied as
    engineering. <strong>Structural</strong> adaptations are body architecture: the
    xerophyte's rolled leaf and sunken stomata that trap humid air against the pores,
    cutting water loss in the desert; the camel's fat hump and heat-tolerant cells; the
    polar bear's hollow translucent fur that channels warmth to black skin. The syllabus's
    classic case is the cactus: leaves reduced to spines (nothing to lose water through,
    nothing to eat), a stem that photosynthesises and stores water, and shallow spreading
    roots that grab brief desert rain. <strong>Physiological</strong> adaptations run
    inside: antifreeze proteins in Arctic fish blood, humans' altitude populations with
    haemoglobin variants that keep oxygen delivery high at 4,000 m. <strong>Behavioural</strong>
    ones are actions: migration, nocturnality in hot climates, basking. A
    <em>common misconception</em>: organisms "develop" adaptations in response to need — a
    giraffe did not stretch and its descendants did not inherit longer necks from the
    stretching. The variation came first (random, undirected); the environment merely
    decided which variants reproduced. Lamark's inheritance-of-acquired-characters was the
    rival theory of Darwin's day, and it is wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>Selection: the filter that does the work</h2>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four steps — simple enough for a poster, deep enough for a century of research" widget="equationStep" config={evoCfg} />

  <p>
    The widget states the mechanism; here it is with numbers, in the fastest laboratory
    evolution offers. A population of bacteria meets an antibiotic. Step 1: variation — a
    random mutation made one cell's enzyme subtly resistant. Step 2: the drug kills
    millions; competition is total. Step 3: the resistant cell survives and divides — every
    20 minutes, not every 20 years. Step 4: within days the entire population descends from
    the mutant, and the drug is useless. Same logic, slower clock, for Darwin's finches:
    the Galápagos drought of 1977 killed the small soft seeds, and within one generation the
    average beak depth in the medium ground finch measurably increased — then shifted back
    in wet years. The Grants' four-decade study watched selection run backwards and
    forwards in real time; beak depth is heritable (h² ≈ 0.65), and the environment is the
    sieve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Selection has three signatures worth naming. <strong>Directional</strong> selection
    pushes the average one way (antibiotics, pesticides, industrial melanism in peppered
    moths — dark forms prevailing in soot-blackened Victorian woods). <strong>Stabilising</strong>
    selection weeds out both extremes (very small or very large human babies face higher
    mortality — which is why birth weight hovers near its optimum). <strong>Disruptive</strong>
    selection favours both extremes against the middle, and is one route toward splitting a
    population in two. Given enough time and isolation — a population split by a river, an
    island, or different mating signals — the two halves accumulate different alleles until
    they can no longer interbreed: that is <strong>speciation</strong>, the branching event
    that fills the tree of life, with the Galápagos finches' thirteen species as the
    textbook case of one colonising lineage splitting into many niches.
  </p>

  <h2>Evidence that built the consensus</h2>
  <p>
    Four independent lines converge. <strong>Fossils in sequence:</strong> whale ancestors
    with legs (<em>Ambulocetus</em>, <em>Rodhocetus</em>), feathered dinosaurs from Chinese
    Lagerstätten, and <em>Tiktaalik</em> — a fish-to-limb transition predicted by where the
    tree said to dig, then found in 2004 in 375-million-year-old Arctic rock.
    <strong>Living evidence:</strong> antibiotic and pesticide resistance evolving within
    seasons, beaks shifting after droughts — evolution observed, not inferred.
    <strong>Shared quirks:</strong> the same bones in whale flipper, bat wing and human
    hand (pentadactyl homology); vestigial wisdom teeth, appendix and the broken
    vitamin-C gene (GULO) we share with other primates — inherited furniture from common
    ancestors, which no designer with freedom to choose would include.
    <strong>Molecular clocks:</strong> DNA divergence between species matches the fossil
    dates independently — two methods, one answer, arrived at with no shared assumptions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "survival of the fittest means strongest." In this theory
    <em>fitness</em> means one thing only: leaving surviving offspring. A slower, weaker,
    uglier variant that raises more young to breeding age is fitter than a magnificent one
    that does not — which is why peacocks' tails, camouflage and antibiotic resistance all
    obey the same arithmetic.
  </p>

  <h2>Speciation and sexual selection: where the branching comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Selection explains change <em>within</em> a lineage; speciation explains the tree's
    forks. The dominant route is <strong>allopatric</strong>: a barrier — a river, a
    canyon, an ocean, an ice sheet — splits a population, and with gene flow cut, the
    halves drift apart under different selections and different luck. Worked case:
    <strong>Darwin's finches</strong> — one ancestral Caribbean-colonising species
    reached Galápagos islands that differ in seed hardness and rainfall; isolated
    beak populations specialised; occasional inter-island colonisation and reinforcement
    (hybrids doing poorly) hardened the differences into species — thirteen of them,
    each a different answer to "what is a seed here?" <strong>Sympatric</strong>
    speciation, without geography, is rarer but real: apple maggot flies switching host
    plants (apples versus hawthorns) now mate on their host's fruit and are diverging in
    the same orchards. The general requirement either way is <strong>reproductive
    isolation</strong> — behavioural (different songs), temporal (different breeding
    seasons), mechanical — and once it holds, the two lineages begin independent
    evolutionary careers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Selection's other face is <strong>sexual selection</strong> — Darwin's own addition,
    needed because the peacock's tail is absurd <em>for survival</em>. Traits that win
    mates can spread against survival cost when mating success varies more than survival:
    stag antlers (male–male combat), bird song (female choice), and the tail itself
    (an honest signal — only a genuinely fit bird can afford the handicap, Zahavi's
    point). The mechanism is the same four steps with a different filter: variation in
    courtship display, differential mating, heritable displays, repetition. It explains
    the most extravagant mismatches between beauty and survival in nature, and it is the
    reason sexual dimorphism tracks mating systems: harem-forming species show huge
    male–female gaps; pair-bonding species barely differ.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The modern synthesis (1930s–50s) bolted Mendelian genetics onto Darwin and made the
    claims quantitative. A population's allele frequencies change only through five forces:
    <strong>selection</strong>, <strong>mutation</strong>, <strong>gene flow</strong>
    (migration between populations), <strong>genetic drift</strong> — the random sampling
    luck that dominates in small populations (the founder effect that filled islands with
    odd species, and why the Amish carry a founder's rare disease allele) — and
    <strong>non-random mating</strong>. Without them, frequencies stay frozen at the
    Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium (p² + 2pq + q² = 1), the null model against which real
    change is measured. Drift's importance was the synthesis's great addition: evolution
    is not all about adaptation; much of the genome's history is random walk, a point the
    neutral theory of molecular evolution (Kimura, 1968) made quantitative.
  </p>
  <p>
    The frontier topics: <strong>evo-devo</strong> shows evolution often works by rewiring
    gene regulation rather than inventing genes — the same toolkits building fly and human
    bodies (Pax6-eye experiments work across phyla). <strong>Kin selection</strong> resolves
    altruism: a gene favouring sacrifice for close kin can spread because relatives share
    it (Hamilton's rule rB > C), the mathematics behind sterile worker insects and alarm
    calls. <strong>Human evolution</strong> is legible in ancient DNA: Neanderthal genes
    make up 1–2% of non-African genomes; lactase persistence spread in the last ~7,000
    years — humans are still evolving, measurably. And the open question with the most at
    stake: how fast can populations adapt to climate change? Corals, trees and polar bears
    are now in a race between generation time and environmental change, and the four-step
    mechanism gives no guarantee the sieve will catch everyone in time.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1859); Grant &amp; Grant, How and Why Species Multiply; Coyne, Why Evolution Is True; Cambridge IGCSE Biology syllabus (section 18); Kimura, The Neutral Theory (1983).</p>
</TopicPage>
